  "text": "The linguistic model used by medieval language theorists relies on the idea that words carry grammatical markers that interact with one another. A phrase is seen as a pairing of two words that share at least one matching marker. This view makes it hard to explain how larger units, such as compounds, can form similar pairings. The present study tries to solve this problem by looking at the role of larger units like clauses and the way grammatical markers might apply to them. To illustrate the argument, the author examines passages from a well‑known medieval philosopher, a contemporary scholar, and an anonymous writer from the same period. An additional section provides a brief overview of the anonymous writer’s contributions."
